From c1d2e3f4a5b60718293a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cole Walker <cole.walker@windriver.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 11:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] port: inhibit_announce must not transition to MASTER on GM
 loss

An inhibit_announce port is, by definition, forbidden from sending
Announce messages. It must therefore never assume the MASTER role: a
"master" that emits no Announce is a contradiction, and downstream
consumers (BMCA peers, notifications, monitoring) that key off port
state see a bogus MASTER during the grandmaster-absent window.

Observed (G.8275.2 PTS T-BC, domain 44, UDPv6 unicast):
  controller-1 ptp11 port enp81s0f1 (inhibit_announce=1) transitions
  SLAVE -> MASTER when the upstream T-GM is lost. It recovers to SLAVE
  when the T-GM returns, but the MASTER holdover state is incorrect.
  Expected: the port holds in LISTENING until an upstream master
  reappears.

Root cause: this is a two-patch composition gap.

  * Patch 0014 (bmc: multi-port BMCA correctness) enforces the
    "inhibit_announce => PS_LISTENING" invariant inside
    bmc_state_decision(). That guard, however, is only consulted for
    BMCA-driven recommended states (the EV_RS_* events produced in
    handle_state_decision_event()).

  * Patch 0017 (port: master loss detection on inhibit_announce ports)
    fixed a genuinely stuck SLAVE port by returning
    EV_ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES directly from the announce
    receipt timeout handler.

On a boundary-clock instance (dataset_comparison=G.8275.x, not
clientOnly, so the port runs ptp_fsm) the ptp_fsm PS_SLAVE handler maps
EV_ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES straight to PS_MASTER. That event
path never re-enters bmc_state_decision(), so the patch 0014 guard is
bypassed and the inhibit_announce port reaches MASTER.

Note this cannot be worked around by configuration on a T-BC:
clientOnly is a clock-global option that forces clockClass 255 and
routes the whole instance through ptp_slave_fsm, which would disable the
boundary clock's downstream master port. linuxptp has no per-port
"never-master" option (serverOnly is the opposite). The invariant must
therefore be enforced in code, at the port level.

Fix: preserve patch 0017's master-loss *detection* (drop the stale
foreign master record, re-arm the announce timer, prune delay requests)
but, instead of returning EV_ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES, request a
fresh state decision (clock_set_sde) and return EV_NONE. BMCA then
re-runs and bmc_state_decision()'s existing inhibit_announce guard
returns PS_LISTENING. This keeps the "inhibit => never master" invariant
in a single place (BMCA) and makes the upstream port behave identically
whether its instance is an OC or a T-BC, and regardless of clientOnly.

Test Plan:
PASS: G.8275.2 PTS T-BC, upstream inhibit_announce port -> LISTENING
  (not MASTER) within announceReceiptTimeout of T-GM loss; downstream
  master port unaffected.
PASS: Port recovers to SLAVE when the T-GM returns.
PASS: Repeated T-GM loss/recovery cycles converge to LISTENING then
  SLAVE each time (no stuck-SLAVE regression of the original bug).
PASS: Single-port PTS slave OC (no clientOnly) -> LISTENING on loss,
  SLAVE on recovery.
PASS: Non-inhibit_announce ports and boundary-clock master legs
  unaffected.
PASS: build-pkgs -c -p linuxptp (trixie)

Story: 2011744
Task: 55099

Signed-off-by: Cole Walker <cole.walker@windriver.com>
---
 port.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/port.c b/port.c
index c3a8b9f..d7e0a12 100644
--- a/port.c
+++ b/port.c
@@ -2925,7 +2925,8 @@ static enum fsm_event bc_event(struct port *p, int fd_index)
 				}
 				port_set_announce_tmo(p);
 				delay_req_prune(p);
-				return EV_ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES;
+				clock_set_sde(p->clock, 1);
+				return EV_NONE;
 			} else {
 				port_clr_tmo(p->fda.fd[FD_ANNOUNCE_TIMER]);
 				clock_set_sde(p->clock, 1);
-- 
